//! SHA-256, so that an approval names **an image** and not merely a file name.
//!
//! # Why a digest at all, and why this one
//!
//! MJXOFF-165's sharpest sentence is that *a golden image generated by the code under test always
//! matches the code under test*. The answer this crate gives is that a baseline is only a baseline
//! once a person has approved it — and an approval that named only the baseline's *name* would be
//! satisfied by any bytes that later appeared under that name, which is exactly the regeneration
//! path it exists to catch. So an approval records the digest of what was approved, and a baseline
//! whose artefacts no longer hash to it is **unapproved**, not merely changed.
//!
//! SHA-256 rather than a fast non-cryptographic hash for one practical reason: a person can check it
//! without this crate. `sha256sum baselines/<name>/render.png` on any machine reproduces the number
//! in the approval record, so the record is auditable by the human whose approval it claims.
//!
//! # Why it is written out here
//!
//! `CLAUDE.md`'s pure-Rust rule and this crate's *"nothing depends on it"* position both point the
//! same way, but the real reason is smaller: this is sixty lines of a published, fixed algorithm
//! with official test vectors, and it is proved against those vectors in
//! `tests/the_instruments_measure_what_they_claim.rs` rather than trusted.

/// The eight initial hash values: the fractional parts of the square roots of the first eight
/// primes, as FIPS 180-4 §5.3.3 gives them.
const INITIAL: [u32; 8] = [
    0x6a09_e667,
    0xbb67_ae85,
    0x3c6e_f372,
    0xa54f_f53a,
    0x510e_527f,
    0x9b05_688c,
    0x1f83_d9ab,
    0x5be0_cd19,
];

